# Emulsion Chemistry
#
# How silver halide crystals in gelatin record light: the latent image, chemical and dye sensitization, tabular grains, the color tripack with its couplers, and the orange mask of color negative.

#>media_carriers_emulsion
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!media_carriers
#:form #!ledger
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone
#:era #!modern

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Emulsion Chemistry"
 ~#claim: "A few silver atoms formed by light make a grain developable, and color negative layers that record with couplers carry an orange mask that corrects their dyes' unwanted absorptions."
 ~#proof: "James for the photographic process; Gurney and Mott 1938 for the latent image; Hunt for color reproduction and masking couplers."
 ~#door: "Read the latent image claim, then the orange mask, then follow the film into the processing machine."
 ~#audience: "cinematographers, photographers, film students, and chemistry readers curious about analog image capture"
 continuity: "Emulsion Chemistry | orange mask"
 cold_open: "A few silver atoms turn a crystal into a picture, and the orange mask on a color negative quietly corrects its imperfect dyes."
}

